---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Newsletter from '../components/Newsletter.astro';
const faq = [
["How much backup power do I need for a week without power after a hurricane?", "Our hurricane scenario (fridge, freezer, ceiling fan, sump pump, Wi-Fi, lights, phones) uses about 9 kWh a day, or about 74 kWh for 7 days with the 0.85 usable factor. That's more than any single portable station holds, so most people pair a battery with solar or a generator."],
["How much gas does a small generator use in a week?", "A Honda EU2200i runs 8.1 hours on 0.95 gallons at 1/4 load, per Honda. Running around the clock at that load is about 2.8 gallons a day, or about 20 gallons for a week. Gas stations can close during outages, so plan fuel before the storm."],
["Can I run a generator during the storm?", "Ready.gov says to keep a generator dry and protected from rain or flooding, and always outdoors at least 20 feet from windows, doors, and attached garages. Many people wait until the wind and rain pass."]
];
---
<Base footerNewsletter={false} title="Hurricane Power Outage: Backup Power for a Week Without Power | HomeBackupCost" description="How to plan backup power for a week-long outage after a hurricane: about 74 kWh for essentials, generator fuel per day, solar recharge, and safety from Ready.gov." schema={[{ '@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'FAQPage', mainEntity: faq.map(([q,a]) => ({ '@type':'Question', name:q, acceptedAnswer:{ '@type':'Answer', text:a } })) }]}>
<h1>Hurricane outages: planning for a week without power</h1>
<p class="lede">What it takes to keep food cold, air moving, and phones charged for 7 days after a storm. Figures as of 2026-10-28.</p>
<p>The Atlantic hurricane season runs June 1–November 30, and Ready.gov notes you may not have access to supplies "for days or even weeks" after a hurricane (<a href="https://www.ready.gov/hurricanes">Ready.gov Hurricanes</a>, updated 10/16/2026). A week is a sensible planning target for coastal and near-coastal homes.</p>

<h2>What a week of essentials takes</h2>
<p><span class="badge est">Estimate</span> Refrigerator, freezer, ceiling fan, 1/3 HP sump pump, Wi-Fi, 6 LED bulbs, and phone charging come to about <strong>9.0 kWh/day</strong>, or about <strong>74 kWh for 7 days</strong> (wattage midpoints from the <a href="https://www.championpowerequipment.com/generator-wattage-chart/">Champion chart</a>, our default hours, ÷ 0.85 usable). <a href="/calculator/?scenario=hurricane">Open the hurricane scenario →</a></p>
<p>Ways to cut it: skip the freezer once it's emptied into coolers, run the sump pump only if water is coming in, and charge devices during the day.</p>

<h2>Three ways to cover a week</h2>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Battery + solar</h3><p>No fuel and no exhaust. <span class="badge est">Assumption</span> Each 400 W panel adds about 1.2 kWh on a sunny day (4 peak sun hours, 75% output), so covering 9 kWh/day takes roughly 8 panels plus a large station. Skies often stay cloudy right after a storm. <a href="/solar-backup/">Solar backup guide →</a></p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Portable generator</h3><p>A Honda EU2200i runs 8.1 h on 0.95 gal at 1/4 load (<a href="https://powerequipment.honda.com/generators/models/eu2200i">Honda</a>): about <strong>2.8 gal/day, or about 20 gal/week</strong> running around the clock. Gas stations can close during outages (<a href="https://www.ready.gov/power-outages">Ready.gov</a>). <a href="/power-station-vs-generator-cost/">Compare costs →</a></p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Standby generator</h3><p>Starts on its own and runs on natural gas or propane. A Generac 22 kW uses 2.53 gal/h of propane at half load (<a href="https://www.generac.com/residential-products/standby-generators/22kW-Standby-Generator-With-Whole-House-Switch-WiFi-Enabled-7043/">Generac</a>). <a href="/standby-generator-installation-cost/">Installed cost →</a></p></div>
</div>

<h2>Before the storm</h2>
<ul>
<li>Charge phones and backup batteries when a hurricane is forecast (<a href="https://www.ready.gov/hurricanes">Ready.gov</a>).</li>
<li>Fully charge power stations and fill fuel before landfall.</li>
<li>Know your evacuation zone. If officials tell you to evacuate, do so; your backup power plan is for after you're cleared to stay or return.</li>
</ul>
<h2>After the storm</h2>
<ul>
<li>Don't touch electrical equipment if it's wet or you're standing in water. If it's safe, turn off power at the main breaker (<a href="https://www.ready.gov/hurricanes">Ready.gov</a>).</li>
<li>Generators: outdoors only, dry, 20+ feet from windows, doors, and attached garages; let them cool before refueling (<a href="https://www.ready.gov/power-outages">Ready.gov</a>). <a href="/generator-safety/">Generator safety →</a></li>
<li>Food: toss anything that's been at 40°F or higher for 2+ hours (<a href="https://www.ready.gov/power-outages">Ready.gov</a>).</li>
</ul>
<h2>FAQ</h2>
{faq.map(([q,a]) => <details class="card" style="margin-bottom:10px"><summary>{q}</summary><p>{a}</p></details>)}
<Newsletter />
</Base>
